As shown in Fig. 5.4, the ratio of mesons over charged pions () is much lower than that of either (anti)protons over charged pions or (anti)lambdas over charged pions (). This is due to the small hadronic cross section of mesons as implemented in RQMD. The -meson shows only little interaction in the hadron gas leading to an early freeze-out time at small transverse radius, as can be seen in Fig. 5.5. The meson might freeze-out as soon as it formed. Therefore, eventual collective motion of the meson might have been dominantly built up during the prehadronic, or in other words, partonic stage. At the maximum SPS energy (), the multistrange baryon might be less sensitive to hadronic rescattering [115]. It seems that multistrange particles freeze-out earlier compared with nonstrange ones. So in order to identify and study partonic collectivity, a systematic study of the distributions of mesons and other multistrange particles, including both elliptical and radial flow, is essential.

